  "content": {
    "title": "Why Missouri’s Dennis Gates doesn’t see St. Louis site as a home game",
    "text": "Updated March 19, 2026, 5:23 p.m. CTMissouri basketball’s players, coaches and support staff gathered on the March Madness logo at center court toward the end of the Tigers’ allotted 30-minute practice on Thursday at Enterprise Center for their team photo.Dennis Gates, Mizzou’s fourth-year head coach, wanted another couple shots. First, he waved away his coaches and got his 14 players to get their own shot. Then, he shouted over to the Mizzou band and cheerleaders to join his team on the floor.While they all assembled, a man in a gold jersey in the front row of the St. Louis-based arena’s stands shouted a familiar three letters:M-I-Z.A group gathered on the opposite side of the arena, oval Tiger emblems etched onto a fair share of their black-and-gold clothes, responded in rhythm. Z-O-U.Missouri’s first game of the NCAA Tournament is in its home state, and even the small crowd that gathered for Thursday’s open practices had a home-state tint. The Tigers, even though they’re the higher seed on the 10 line, will take on 7-seed Miami in the Round of 64 of the NCAA Tournament on Friday night in St. Louis, just 125 miles from Mizzou’s home court.A good deal closer than Coral Gables, Florida.But not necessarily a home game, Gates said.“I don't view anything as a home game because it is not at Mizzou Arena on Norm Stewart Court,” Gates told reporters. “I can't project the ticket sales. We look at it as the NCAA Tournament. And the NCAA Tournament is all about neutral sites. That's why the growth of MTEs took place, so you can have a postseason sort of experience in a preseason. Once you get selected and once you get the site you are supposed to go to.“For us we are looking at it as a game at a neutral-site court. Purdue travels. So I am sure their fans are going to buy a lot of tickets. Queens, first-time tournament team, Coach has done a tremendous job there, they are excited. They are going to buy a lot of tickets.”Mizzou is playing in the evening window Friday night. The Tigers’ planned 9:10 p.m. CDT tipoff at Enterprise Center will follow 2-seed Purdue versus 15-seed Queens. Fans who buy Session 2 tickets can attend both games.Playing in your home seed as a 10-seed is rare. It hasn’t happened since 2008. And, although it garnered some online attention for what seems to be a disadvantage for Miami, it’s not really something that the selection committee considers this far down the bracket.Indeed, Purdue is the team that likely asked to be in St. Louis. The Boilermakers, as a 2-seed, have protected seeding, and St. Louis is geographically closest to their Indiana campus. The NCAA can’t put them at a geographical disadvantage. And the Purdue fanbase tends to travel well.“So whatever allotment that was given to us, I know it is not the entire arena. I do know that,” Gates said. “We will see what it looks like, what it sounds like. But I am excited about the opportunity, that's how I see it. And our guys are excited about being a participant in this tournament and our staff are excited as well.”Miami appears to have leaned into the “road game” element, even if Gates and the Tigers aren’t calling it a home matchup.Hurricanes head coach Jai Lucas told CBS Sports earlier this week that the Hurricanes would “play on the moon” if that’s where they were selected.Lucas was asked again about the trip Thursday in his pregame press conference.“We haven't really been respected to the level that we think we should be,” Lucas said. “Where we were projected to be in the ACC, the projections (at the) beginning of the year, now being a tournament team. Now you get all of this stuff and get all of this credit and praise, and then you get a seventh seed and have to go on the road.“So for us it is business as usual. We won eight road games. We are expecting it to be like a road game. We are (treating it) like being a real road game. We have to go and earn and take our respect like we have done the whole year.”Mizzou has already played at Enterprise Center, losing 91-48 in the Braggin’ Rights game with a shorthanded roster against Illinois in December.The Tigers have mostly steered the ship back toward safety since then, but they are riding a three-game losing streak into the Big Dance.In all likelihood, the crowd Friday will have a Missouri lean. It did Thursday during the open practices, even in a mostly empty arena.But Mizzou isn’t leaning on crowd size.“Being this close to home is pretty cool having the home-crowd advantage,” MU forward Mark Mitchell said. “A tournament is a tournament. The location can't play for us.”",
